data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing modifies the genomes of living organisms and is based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- Cas9 nuclease, complexed with a synthetic guide RNA (gRNA), is delivered into a cell, which can cut the genome at a desired location [1].\n- Genome editing inserts, deletes, modifies, or replaces DNA at site-specific locations, using programmable \"molecular scissors\" that create double-strand breaks [3].\n- In bacteria, CRISPR sequences derive from bacteriophage DNA and help detect and destroy similar phages during later infections [2].\n- Applications named in headlines: a story titled as a breakthrough for treating a broad array of diseases [4], and a report that HIV in cell culture was completely eliminated using CRISPR-Cas editing [5]. These are headline-level only, so the full findings are unverified."}

